## Migration: .NET Framework → .NET 8

### Problems with the original code on .NET 8

| API | Issue |
|-----|-------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Unreliable; obsolete in .NET 9 (SYSLIB0046) |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Can't be woken by cancellation token |
| Race condition | Multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose items |

### Pattern classification

- **Cancellable work loop** — the `while(true)` in `RunLoop`
- **Blocking call interruption** — `WaitOne()` needs to wake on stop

### Migrated code

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _runTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — nothing to do
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();  // Signal no more items

        // Give the loop time to drain, then force-cancel
        using var timeoutCts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
        using var linked = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(
            _cts.Token, timeoutCts.Token);

        try
        {
            if (_runTask is not null)
                await _runTask.WaitAsync(linked.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            _cts.Cancel(); // Force cancel if timeout exceeded
            if (_runTask is not null)
                await _runTask.ConfigureAwait(ConfigureAwaitOptions.SuppressThrowing);
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

### Key changes explained

| Before | After | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `Thread` + `ManualResetEvent` | `Channel<T>` + `Task.Run` | Thread-safe producer/consumer with backpressure, no manual locking |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` | Cooperative cancellation — the only model in .NET 6+ |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Channel.Writer.Complete()` | Signals graceful shutdown; `ReadAllAsync` exits naturally |
| `lock` + `Queue<T>` | `Channel<T>` | Lock-free, handles multiple producers correctly |
| Race (multiple signals lost) | Channel buffers all items | Fixes the original bug where rapid enqueues lost events |

### Pitfalls to watch for

1. **`Thread.Abort` → `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — This is the blocking issue. There is no workaround; you must switch to cooperative cancellation.

2. **`Handle()` must cooperate** — If `Handle` does long-running work, pass the `CancellationToken` into it and check periodically, otherwise `Stop` may hang until the current item finishes.

3. **Dispose the `CancellationTokenSource`** — Leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. The `IDisposable` pattern ensures cleanup.

4. **Synchronous callers** — If you can't make `Stop` async, use:
   ```csharp
   public void Stop() => StopAsync().GetAwaiter().GetResult();
   ```
   But prefer async all the way up.

5. **Don't catch and swallow `OperationCanceledException`** — Only catch it at the top-level loop. Let it propagate elsewhere so callers know work was cancelled.
